# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROS 2 Jazzy + Gazebo Harmonic (gz-sim 8), Ubuntu 24.04 Noble.
#
# This supersedes docker/deprecated/Dockerfile.gazebo-harmonic, which was the
# same simulator on Humble. The simulator did not change. The distro did.
#
# Why Jazzy, verified against the REP 2000 source on 2026-09-15:
#       Humble Hawksbill   May 2022 - May 2027
#       Jazzy Jalisco      May 2024 - May 2029
#   The competition is 4 to 8 June 2027. Humble reaches end of life the month
#   before it. That is the same objection that ruled out Gazebo Fortress, and
#   it applies with equal force to the distro underneath. Jazzy also covers the
#   2028 team, which matters for a group with annual turnover.
#
# Why Harmonic is still the right simulator, and now more so:
#   REP 2000 lists Fortress as Humble's pairing and Harmonic as Jazzy's. The
#   old image ran Harmonic on Humble deliberately, but off-label. Here the
#   pairing is the supported one, so we keep every result already measured
#   (GPU render path, bridge, camera budget) and stop swimming upstream.
#
# What this deletes, relative to the Humble image:
#   The whole packages.osrfoundation.org block. Jazzy's ros_gz and its Gazebo
#   come from packages.ros.org itself, through the ros-jazzy-gz-*-vendor
#   packages. No third-party apt key, no extra source list, and no
#   Fortress/Harmonic flavour split - ros-jazzy-ros-gzharmonic and
#   ros-jazzy-ros-gzfortress do not exist, because there is nothing to
#   disambiguate. The Debian Conflicts problem documented for Humble is gone.
#
# What this ADDS, and it is the important one:
#   ros-jazzy-gz-ros2-control 1.2.20 EXISTS as a binary, checked against the
#   live noble index on 2026-09-15. On Humble this package was built against
#   libignition-gazebo6 (Fortress despite the gz- name) and installing it would
#   have dragged a second Gazebo into the image, so it was the one genuine
#   source build blocking Stage 2. On Jazzy it depends on the same
#   ros-jazzy-gz-*-vendor packages as ros_gz. The source build is gone.
#
#   Installing it is NOT the same as adopting it. RQ-06 - gz_ros2_control
#   versus a grr_hardware/GazeboDriveHardware topic bridge - is still open and
#   still a team decision. It is installed here only so that trying the
#   ros2_control path costs nothing.
#
# Base image choice: ros:jazzy-ros-base, not osrf/ros:jazzy-desktop, which the
# team standardised on for DevEnv. Desktop is roughly 3.5 GB against 800 MB and
# this is a simulator image, not a dev environment. Disk is a live constraint
# here - docker_data.vhdx never shrinks and Windows 11 Home has no Hyper-V to
# compact it. rviz2 and xacro are installed explicitly below, so nothing that
# the desktop image would have provided is actually missing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM ros:jazzy-ros-base

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# Ask the NVIDIA container runtime for the GRAPHICS capability, not just
# compute. Without this the default is utility,compute, the GL/EGL driver
# libraries are never injected, and ogre2 silently falls back to llvmpipe
# software rendering while every topic keeps publishing at a plausible rate.
# That silent fallback is the dangerous failure mode from RQ-02 and it is
# distro-independent - it will bite on Jazzy exactly as it did on Humble.
ENV NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=all
ENV NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES=all

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gazebo Harmonic and the ROS 2 bridge, both from packages.ros.org.
#
# ros-jazzy-ros-gz is the metapackage: ros_gz_sim, ros_gz_bridge, ros_gz_image
# and ros_gz_interfaces. Gazebo itself arrives as its dependency chain of
# ros-jazzy-gz-*-vendor packages.
#
# ros-jazzy-simulation-interfaces is message definitions only, installed so
# igvc_simulation_interface still compiles. Per RQ-05 there is no Gazebo server
# on Harmonic that answers those services; that landed in Jetty.
#
# ros-jazzy-teleop-twist-keyboard is how you actually drive the thing. The
# repo's existing teleop.launch.py does NOT work here: it wants a physical
# joystick at /dev/input/js0, pulls in motor_controllers.launch.py with the
# CanInterface ros2_control stack that Stage 1 deliberately does not use, and
# remaps to /diff_drive_controller/cmd_vel rather than the /cmd_vel the
# built-in DiffDrive plugin listens on. Keyboard teleop needs no hardware and
# talks straight to /cmd_vel.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        ros-jazzy-ros-gz \
        ros-jazzy-gz-ros2-control \
        ros-jazzy-simulation-interfaces \
        ros-jazzy-xacro \
        ros-jazzy-robot-state-publisher \
        ros-jazzy-rviz2 \
        ros-jazzy-teleop-twist-keyboard \
        mesa-utils \
        vulkan-tools \
        python3-colcon-common-extensions \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Read by gz_ros2_control and by anything that source-builds against Gazebo.
ENV GZ_VERSION=harmonic

WORKDIR /root/ros2_ws
